<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rambagh Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rambagh Palace is a former royal residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. Built in 1835 as a garden house and later enlarged into a palace, it became the main residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Man Singh II in 1925, and in 1957 it was converted into a luxury hotel, one of the first palace hotels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and a model for the heritage tourism of Rajasthan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rambagh-palace.jpg" alt="Rambagh Palace" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rambagh_Palace_hotel_Jaipur_lobby_courtyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first building in the Rambagh Gardens, south of the old city and about 5 miles (8.0 km) outside its walls, was a garden house for the wet nurse of Prince Ram Singh II. Maharaja Sawai Ram Singh II later enlarged it for Kesar Badaran, his maid and an attendant of his queen. At first it was a simple pavilion on the forested outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, and by the late 19th century it served as a royal hunting lodge and guesthouse for relatives and visitors hunting in the surrounding country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the early 20th century the lodge was rebuilt as a palace in the Indo-Saracenic style to designs by Sir Samuel Swinton Jacob, blending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and Mughal forms. Built mainly of pale pink sandstone with marble detail, it has chhatris, carved stone balconies, jali screens, bracketed eaves and arched verandahs with scalloped openings around its courtyards. Inside are hand-painted ceilings, stucco cornices and Venetian glass chandeliers. Its 47 acres of gardens follow Mughal formal patterns, with geometric flowerbeds, promenades, reflecting pools, fountains and lotus ponds, and peacocks still roam the lawns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Maharaja Sawai Man Singh II, who ruled from 1922 to 1970, added royal suites and lived there with Maharani Gayatri Devi. His coronation was held there in 1931, and the palace was the setting for royal weddings, polo matches, balls and the stays of dignitaries; by some accounts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a>, was entertained there in 1950.</p><p>After independence in 1947 and the integration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, the maharaja refitted the royal chambers for guests and opened the palace as a hotel. On 2 May 1972 its management passed to Taj Hotels, which reduced the number of rooms and modernised its plumbing and air-conditioning while keeping its historic character. Guests have included Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Louis Mountbatten</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, and the palace has served as a film location, for Lamhe (1991) and Bajirao Mastani (2015).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rambagh Palace, Jaipur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…rulers did not disappear: many entered politics, among them Gayatri Devi of Jaipur, elected to the Lok Sabha in 1962, and several turned palaces into hotels, starting with Jaipur's <b>Rambagh Palace</b> in 1957, helping to make Rajasthan's royal heritage the basis of one of India's main tourist industries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
